     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "In nineteen fifty, a tiny robotic mouse wound through a physical maze. You think AI was a sudden breakthrough from modern tech giants, but it actually started decades earlier. Early engineers used basic logic to solve physical paths long before modern chips, because they wanted machines to think. In nineteen fifty-two, Grace Hopper invented the first computer compiler called A-0 on the UNIVAC one. Programmers had to write instructions entirely in numeric machine code until her compiler translated words into commands. By twenty twenty-three, autonomous agents like AgentGPT started making independent decisions without any human hands. My own experiments show that machines stopped just following paths and started choosing them. The robotic mouse of nineteen fifty just wanted cheese, but it built the mind we use today."
    }
   }
